Analysis Plan:
1. Compute basic per-cell spatial crowding and same-type neighborhood metrics (e.g., distance to k-th nearest neighbor overall, fraction of same-population neighbors, and local cell density) from adata.obsm['spatial'] and summarize their distributions per Population and Sample_ID without generating any figures, only tabular/text outputs.
2. Within key cardiac populations (e.g., major ventricular and atrial cardiomyocyte subtypes and fibroblast populations), quantify the association between Purity/Complexity and each spatial metric using per-population linear models that adjust for Sample_ID and UMI Count, reporting effect sizes, standard errors, and p-values.
3. Assess whether the strength and direction of these spatial–maturation associations are consistent across samples by repeating the regressions within each Sample_ID and meta-analyzing or comparing estimates textually across samples.
4. Test whether cells in the top vs bottom quantiles of Purity or Complexity within each focal population differ in spatial clustering (e.g., same-type neighbor fraction and local density), using non-parametric tests (e.g., Mann–Whitney U) and report effect sizes and p-values.
5. Explore whether spatial heterogeneity in maturation is anisotropic within each sample by correlating Purity and Complexity with raw spatial coordinates and simple spatial gradients (e.g., along x or y axes), using Spearman correlations and permutation tests to assess significance.
